infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title IPv4 vs IPv6: same envelope, different scale
  desc The three differences that actually bite when a connection fails or a deployment must run dual-stack
  compares
    - label IPv4
      children
        - label 32-bit address (4 bytes)
          desc Four decimal octets, e.g. 93.184.216.34
        - label ~4.3 billion addresses — exhausted
          desc The whole space is allocated; NAT papers over the shortage
        - label Variable header, checksum per hop
          desc 20-byte minimum; every router recomputes the header checksum and may fragment
    - label IPv6
      children
        - label 128-bit address (16 bytes)
          desc Eight 16-bit hex groups, e.g. 2001:db8::1
        - label 340 undecillion — effectively unlimited
          desc Address space ends the exhaustion crisis; ~45-50% of global traffic by 2026
        - label Fixed 40-byte header, no checksum
          desc Checksum delegated to L2/L4; no router-level fragmentation, so forwarding is simpler
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
